---
Part XI, Chapter 4: Installing the executable
Package: ./
Language: sh
---

# Installing the executable

A person without Node or Bun installs Loom with one line in a terminal:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://github.com/athrio-com/loom/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh
```

`curl` downloads the install script, and `sh` runs it. The script picks the executable this machine needs from the latest release and checks it against the release's list of checksums. It puts the executable in a folder Loom owns, `~/.loom/bin`, and adds that folder to `PATH`, the list of folders a terminal searches for a command. Every release carries the script beside the executables, so the line above always runs the script of the latest release. When Loom's website is live, `loomframework.com/install.sh` can redirect to the same address.

A person who installs Loom should not have to fix `PATH` by hand afterwards. So the script edits the shell's profile, the file a shell reads as it starts, the way the installers of Bun, Deno and Rust do. It also looks for any other `loom` already on `PATH`, such as one an npm install left behind, and names the command that removes it.

The script covers macOS and Linux. Windows has no `sh` to run it with, so a Windows user downloads `loom-windows-x64.exe.gz` from the release page and unpacks it instead.

## Stopping at the first failure

The script is written for `sh`, the shell every Unix system carries. It uses nothing that Bash adds to `sh`, since `sh` on Debian and Ubuntu is Dash, a smaller shell without those additions. It stops at the first command that fails, and at the first variable it reads that was never set, so a broken step never leaves a half-installed Loom behind. `fail` prints why it stopped and exits non-zero.

~>

#!/bin/sh
# Installs Loom's standalone executable and puts it on PATH.
#
#   curl -fsSL https://github.com/athrio-com/loom/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh
#
# Pass a version to install that release instead of the latest:
#
#   curl -fsSL …/install.sh | sh -s -- 0.0.13
#
# The executable goes into ~/.loom/bin, or the folder LOOM_INSTALL_DIR names, and the
# script adds that folder to PATH in your shell's profile. Pass --no-modify-path, or set
# LOOM_NO_MODIFY_PATH=1, to leave every profile alone.

set -eu

fail() {
  printf 'loom install: %s\n' "$1" >&2
  exit 1
}

~

## What the person asks for

The script takes two kinds of argument. A version picks the release to install, with or without its leading `v`. The `--no-modify-path` option leaves every profile alone, for a person who manages `PATH` themselves. `LOOM_NO_MODIFY_PATH` does the same from the environment, when it holds any value, for a setup script that cannot pass an argument through a pipe. Anything else that starts with a dash is a mistake, and the script stops on it rather than guess.

~>

version=
modify_path=yes
if [ -n "${LOOM_NO_MODIFY_PATH:-}" ]; then
  modify_path=no
fi

for argument in "$@"; do
  case $argument in
    --no-modify-path) modify_path=no ;;
    -*) fail "unknown option $argument" ;;
    *) version=${argument#v} ;;
  esac
done

~

## The executable this machine needs

The script names the file it needs the way the release names them, `loom-<system>-<architecture>.gz`, an executable gzipped to a third of its size. `uname` reports the system and the architecture. Linux on ARM reports `aarch64` and macOS on Apple silicon reports `arm64`, and both get the `arm64` executable.

A terminal that runs under Rosetta, Apple's translator for Intel programs, reports `x86_64` even on Apple silicon. The Intel executable would run there, but more slowly, so the script asks macOS whether it is being translated, and takes the native executable when it is.

~>

case "$(uname -s)" in
  Darwin) system=darwin ;;
  Linux) system=linux ;;
  *) fail "there is no executable for $(uname -s); on Windows, download loom-windows-x64.exe from https://github.com/athrio-com/loom/releases" ;;
esac

case "$(uname -m)" in
  x86_64 | amd64) architecture=x64 ;;
  arm64 | aarch64) architecture=arm64 ;;
  *) fail "there is no executable for the $(uname -m) architecture" ;;
esac

if [ "$system" = darwin ] && [ "$architecture" = x64 ] &&
  [ "$(sysctl -n sysctl.proc_translated 2>/dev/null || true)" = 1 ]; then
  architecture=arm64
fi

asset="loom-$system-$architecture.gz"

~

## Where the release lives

A release's files sit at one address on GitHub, and the latest release has an address of its own that always points at the newest. A version given to the script picks that release's address instead. `LOOM_DOWNLOAD` overrides the address outright, which is how the smoke test serves a release of its own.

~>

if [ -n "$version" ]; then
  release="https://github.com/athrio-com/loom/releases/download/v$version"
else
  release="https://github.com/athrio-com/loom/releases/latest/download"
fi
release="${LOOM_DOWNLOAD:-$release}"

~

## A folder of Loom's own

The executable goes into `~/.loom/bin`, a folder Loom owns, as Bun keeps its own in `~/.bun/bin` and Deno in `~/.deno/bin`. A folder of its own means the install never writes into a folder another program manages, and removing Loom means removing one folder. `LOOM_INSTALL_DIR` names another folder instead. A `.loom` folder in the home folder makes no workspace of it, since a workspace is a folder that holds `.loom/config.yaml`.

The folder may already hold a `loom`. One that an earlier run of this script put there is Loom's executable, and the install replaces it. Anything else there is not the script's to replace, such as a script a person wrote to switch between versions of Loom. So the script looks before it downloads anything. A link is not Loom's executable. Neither is a script, a file whose first line opens with `#!` to name the program that runs it, which a compiled executable never does. Neither is a program that does not answer `--version` the way Loom does. In each case the script names the file, leaves it as it was, and stops.

~>

dir="${LOOM_INSTALL_DIR:-$HOME/.loom/bin}"

if [ -e "$dir/loom" ] || [ -L "$dir/loom" ]; then
  if [ -L "$dir/loom" ] || [ "$(head -c 2 "$dir/loom")" = '#!' ] ||
    ! "$dir/loom" --version 2>/dev/null | grep -q '^loom v'; then
    fail "$dir/loom is not a Loom executable, so the install leaves it alone; move it out of $dir, then run this again"
  fi
fi

~

## A download that checks out

The script downloads the gzipped executable and the list of checksums into a temporary folder, which it removes however it exits. It then computes the download's SHA-256 checksum, with `sha256sum` on Linux and `shasum` on macOS, and compares it with the one the list gives. A file that does not match stops the install before anything reaches the person's `PATH`. The check catches a download that was cut short or corrupted. It cannot prove who built the file, since the list comes from the same place. Once the checksum matches, `gunzip` unpacks the executable beside the download.

~>

work=$(mktemp -d)
trap 'rm -rf "$work"' EXIT

curl -fsSL "$release/$asset" -o "$work/$asset" || fail "could not download $release/$asset"
curl -fsSL "$release/SHA256SUMS" -o "$work/SHA256SUMS" ||
  fail "could not download $release/SHA256SUMS"

expected=$(awk -v name="$asset" '$2 == name || $2 == "*" name { print $1 }' "$work/SHA256SUMS")
[ -n "$expected" ] || fail "SHA256SUMS lists no $asset"

if command -v sha256sum >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  actual=$(sha256sum "$work/$asset" | awk '{ print $1 }')
else
  actual=$(shasum -a 256 "$work/$asset" | awk '{ print $1 }')
fi
[ "$actual" = "$expected" ] || fail "the checksum of $asset does not match SHA256SUMS"

gunzip -c "$work/$asset" > "$work/loom" || fail "could not unpack $asset"

~

## Into place

The executable arrives in two moves. The first carries it into the folder under a temporary name, which may copy it across file systems. The second renames it to `loom` within the folder, in one step, so a `loom` that is already running keeps its file and a new run finds the new one.

~>

mkdir -p "$dir"
chmod 755 "$work/loom"
mv "$work/loom" "$dir/loom.partial.$$"
mv "$dir/loom.partial.$$" "$dir/loom"

printf 'Installed %s at %s\n' "$("$dir/loom" --version)" "$dir/loom"

~

## Putting the folder on PATH

No system puts `~/.loom/bin` on `PATH` by itself, so the script adds the folder to the profile of the person's shell. `SHELL` names that shell, and each shell reads a different profile.

Zsh, the shell macOS starts by default, reads `.zshrc` in the folder `ZDOTDIR` names, or in the home folder. Fish reads `config.fish` in the `fish` folder of the configuration folder, `~/.config` unless `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` names another. Fish's own `fish_add_path` adds a folder to `PATH` once, however often the profile runs. Bash on Linux reads `.bashrc` in every terminal it opens. Bash on macOS starts as a login shell, which reads the first of `.bash_profile`, `.bash_login` and `.profile` that exists, so the script writes to that one, and creates `.bash_profile` when none exists. Any other shell gets no profile, and the person adds the line themselves.

The line a profile receives names the folder through `$HOME` when the folder is inside the home folder, so the profile still works if the home folder moves.

~>

case $dir in
  "$HOME"/*) spelled="\$HOME/${dir#"$HOME"/}" ;;
  *) spelled=$dir ;;
esac

profile=
line="export PATH=\"$spelled:\$PATH\""
case $(basename "${SHELL:-sh}") in
  zsh) profile="${ZDOTDIR:-$HOME}/.zshrc" ;;
  bash)
    if [ "$system" = darwin ]; then
      for candidate in .bash_profile .bash_login .profile; do
        if [ -z "$profile" ] && [ -f "$HOME/$candidate" ]; then
          profile="$HOME/$candidate"
        fi
      done
      profile="${profile:-$HOME/.bash_profile}"
    else
      profile="$HOME/.bashrc"
    fi
    ;;
  fish)
    profile="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/fish/config.fish"
    line="fish_add_path \"$spelled\""
    ;;
esac

~

A folder already on `PATH` needs nothing. Otherwise the script appends the line, under a comment that names Loom, and says which profile it changed. A shell reads its profile only as it starts, so the change reaches the next terminal, and the script says to open one.

The script adds the line only once. A profile that already names the folder, by its full path or through `$HOME`, is left as it is, so running the script again never repeats the line. That covers a person who added the folder by hand, and a terminal opened before the first install that still runs on the old `PATH`. With `--no-modify-path`, or for a shell with no profile the script knows, the script prints the line for the person to add.

~>

case ":$PATH:" in
  *":$dir:"*) ;;
  *)
    if [ "$modify_path" = no ] || [ -z "$profile" ]; then
      printf "%s is not on your PATH. Add this line to your shell's profile:\n  %s\n" "$dir" "$line"
    elif [ -f "$profile" ] && grep -qF -e "$dir" -e "$spelled" "$profile"; then
      printf '%s already adds %s to PATH. Open a new terminal to use loom.\n' "$profile" "$dir"
    else
      mkdir -p "$(dirname "$profile")"
      printf '\n# Loom\n%s\n' "$line" >> "$profile"
      printf 'Added %s to PATH in %s. Open a new terminal to use loom.\n' "$dir" "$profile"
    fi
    ;;
esac

~

## Another loom on PATH

A person may have installed Loom before, with npm or Bun, or with an older script. That `loom` stays where it was, and while it sits on `PATH` it may be the one a terminal runs. The script never removes it, since the person may still want it. It names it, says who installed it, and gives the command that removes it.

Each installer leaves a mark in the path of the files it writes. A `loom` on `PATH` is usually a link to the installed package, so `resolved` follows the link, and each link after it, to the file it leads to.

~>

resolved() {
  target=$1
  hops=0
  while [ -L "$target" ] && [ "$hops" -lt 40 ]; do
    link=$(readlink "$target")
    case $link in
      /*) target=$link ;;
      *) target="$(dirname "$target")/$link" ;;
    esac
    hops=$((hops + 1))
  done
  printf '%s\n' "$target"
}

~

`recognise` reads the marks in the path that `resolved` gives, as `loom doctor` does. It sets `described`, which says who installed the `loom`, and `removal`, the command that removes it. Homebrew keeps its packages under a `Cellar` folder. Bun keeps the packages it installs for the whole machine in a `.bun` folder, and pnpm in a `pnpm` folder.

npm keeps both a project's packages and its own in a `node_modules` folder. A project's sits beside the project's `package.json`, and npm's own sits in a folder with none. So a path through `node_modules` belongs to a project when the folder above the first `node_modules` holds a `package.json`. The project's lockfile then names its package manager, and the removal runs in the project's folder. A path with none of these marks belongs to no installer the script knows. It may be an older standalone executable, and its removal deletes the file.

~>

project_manager() {
  if [ -f "$1/bun.lock" ] || [ -f "$1/bun.lockb" ]; then
    echo bun
  elif [ -f "$1/pnpm-lock.yaml" ]; then
    echo pnpm
  elif [ -f "$1/yarn.lock" ]; then
    echo yarn
  else
    echo npm
  fi
}

recognise() {
  target=$(resolved "$1")
  case $target in
    */Cellar/*)
      described="installed by Homebrew"
      removal="brew uninstall loom"
      ;;
    */.bun/*)
      described="installed by Bun"
      removal="bun remove -g @athrio/loom"
      ;;
    */pnpm/*)
      described="installed by pnpm"
      removal="pnpm remove -g @athrio/loom"
      ;;
    */node_modules/*)
      root=${target%%/node_modules/*}
      if [ -f "$root/package.json" ]; then
        manager=$(project_manager "$root")
        described="installed by $manager in the project at $root"
        if [ "$manager" = npm ]; then
          removal="cd \"$root\" && npm uninstall @athrio/loom"
        else
          removal="cd \"$root\" && $manager remove @athrio/loom"
        fi
      else
        described="installed by npm"
        removal="npm uninstall -g @athrio/loom"
      fi
      ;;
    *)
      described="a file no installer owns"
      removal="rm \"$1\""
      ;;
  esac
}

~

The script then walks `PATH` folder by folder and reports every `loom` it finds there that is not the one it just installed. A folder may appear on `PATH` twice, and two folders may link to one file, so the walk reports each file once. It skips any `loom` that leads to the new one.

~>

seen=
printf '%s\n' "$PATH" | tr ':' '\n' | while IFS= read -r folder; do
  if [ -z "$folder" ] || [ ! -f "$folder/loom" ] || [ ! -x "$folder/loom" ]; then
    continue
  fi
  target=$(resolved "$folder/loom")
  case ":$seen:" in
    *":$target:"*) continue ;;
  esac
  seen="$seen:$target"
  if [ "$target" = "$dir/loom" ]; then
    continue
  fi
  recognise "$folder/loom"
  printf '\nAnother loom is on your PATH, at %s, %s. Remove it with:\n  %s\n' \
    "$folder/loom" "$described" "$removal"
done

~

